  data-description='API-driven blog case study focused on dynamic posts, authentication, admin publishing tools, responsive content, and branded interface design.'
  data-case='agilitybandits'
  aria-labelledby='case-agilitybandits-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Web Development</p>
        <h1 id='case-agilitybandits-title'>Agility Bandits</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>A branded, API-driven blog with public reading and authenticated
          publishing tools.</p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Brand, UX/UI, and front-end development</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Individual Project Exam 1</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2024</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Noroff API</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Complete publishing workflow</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='agilitybandits'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='Agility Bandits sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='agilitybandits'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        The task was to create a responsive blog for a dog-agility brand using API-managed content.
        Visitors needed a clear way to discover and read posts, while an authenticated administrator
        needed practical tools for creating, editing, and managing that content.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Public editorial content and private admin actions had to coexist in the same site.
            Because post titles, images, excerpts, and body copy come from an API, the layout also
            had to remain stable when the real content differed from the original design examples.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Separate public reading from authenticated publishing actions</li>
            <li>Design flexible cards and pages for variable API content</li>
            <li>Keep a playful brand personality without reducing readability</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--homepage'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-agilitybandits-600.webp'
            data-src='site-agilitybandits-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='400'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Agility Bandits desktop blog homepage showing branded navigation and dynamically loaded posts'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Branded homepage with dynamic blog content</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Used semantic HTML, structured CSS, and focused JavaScript modules to keep the content
              hierarchy clear across listing, detail, and admin views
            </li>
            <li>
              Kept posts publicly readable while showing create and edit actions only when the
              administrator has an authenticated session
            </li>
            <li>
              Built responsive content patterns that can accommodate changing image proportions,
              title lengths, and article content from the API
            </li>
            <li>
              Carried the dog-agility identity through color, typography, and custom graphics while
              keeping navigation and controls familiar
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--admin'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-agilitybanditsmobile-600.webp'
            data-src='site-agilitybanditsmobile-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='488'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Agility Bandits mobile homepage and authenticated edit-post interface'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Mobile reading and edit-post views</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The finished project combines a public responsive blog with an authenticated
        content-management journey. It strengthened my foundation in vanilla JavaScript, API
        integration, authentication, and rendering unpredictable content. It also gave me practical
        experience carrying a visual identity through a functional product rather than treating
        branding and development as separate tasks.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'><use href='#i-arrow-left' /></svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
